{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And the sons of Gad said, etc., saying: Your servants will do, etc.</b> It is difficult: (1) the verse's saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — to whom would Moses say [it]? (2) What is the novelty in their words, <b>Your servants will do</b>, when they had already preceded and said what Moses commanded them? (3) After they said in general terms, ״Your servants will do as my master commands,״ why were they required to say further <b>our little ones</b>, etc., <b>shall be there</b>, etc., <b>and your servants shall cross over</b>? And further, why did they repeat, saying <b>as my master speaks</b>, when they had already said, ״Your servants will do as my master commands״? (4) Why did they change — in the first verse they said <b>מְצַוֶּה</b> [commands], and in the second verse they said <b>דּוֹבֵר</b> [speaks]?" "<b>Indeed</b>, according to what I explained in the words of Moses — that he intended to introduce to them anew that their intention in crossing over for the war should be according to the way of those who love His name, as the Rambam wrote in chapter 7 of Hilkhot Melakhim, and also regarding the specific matter of prioritizing cities for the children over building sheepfolds, as they said of blessed memory (Bemidbar Rabbah here) that they cherished money more than children, and Moses reversed it for them — therefore they answered together as one man, ״And the sons of Gad said,״ and they agreed to Moses' statement in both particulars that Moses introduced to them anew, and they began from what is adjacent, which is the matter of cherishing their children above their wealth, and they said <b>our little ones</b>, etc., <b>and all our livestock</b> — see how they put their children before their livestock, in accordance with the words of Moses. And afterward they also declared their acceptance in the first particular, that their intention in the war would be before HaShem, and that is their saying, ״And your servants shall cross over, every armed man of the army, before HaShem, as my master speaks״ — and not as they had said at first, ״We will hasten, armed, before the children of Israel until we bring them,״ etc." "<b>And behold</b>, because these two matters carry an obligation to fulfill them, since Moses stipulated this upon them, and every condition involving monetary matters is binding even if the one who sets the condition is an ordinary person — therefore they were clever at the beginning of their words and said that even apart from the obligation of the condition, they are bound to fulfill all the words of Moses, the prophet of HaShem, as it is written (Deuteronomy 18:15), ״To him you shall listen.״ And this is what they intended in their first statement, when they said, <b>Your servants will do as my master commands</b> us to do — even without the reason that it is a condition for receiving the land. And therefore the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> is used, meaning: by your word alone there is an obligation upon us to fulfill your commandment." "<b>Furthermore</b>, it intends an expression of exaltation and elevation, for this matter the sons of Gad and the sons of Reuben said on account of the eminence of [Moses as] king and prophet, and afterward they explained the matter and said, <b>Your servants will do as my master speaks</b> — meaning, even apart from the obligation that comes from the mitzvah, there is an obligation upon us to do it, even if the matter were not a mitzvah but merely speech — like matters that pass between a person and his fellow in matters of purchase and sale conditions, which are valid and confer validity in worldly affairs. And the intention in this is that they also accept upon themselves the matter like those things by which a person becomes obligated to his fellow through monetary conditions, which are as valid as cast metal."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The word לֵאמֹר is here interpreted as from the root אמר in the sense of speech/word, rendered as 'by your word.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:00.379240319Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}